#Managing Security Credentials (Access Key and Secret Key):
•To let AWS know our identity and also let Terraform know exactly where we want to create the resources, we use an Access Key and a Secret Key.
•Setting Environment Variables

#On a Local Windows Machine: Add these two environment variables locally via the System Environment Variables settings.

#On a Linux VM: We can set them directly in the terminal session using the export command:

export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID="your_access_key"
export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="your_secret_key"

* Security Warning: In a Linux VM, we can add them as environment variables or directly inside the script itself—which is highly discouraged.
Why so? Because if the script is shared or accessed by anyone else, it exposes your raw credentials, creating a massive security threat. Using export via the terminal temporarily sets the environment variables for your active session only.

#Step-by-Step: Creating AWS Security Credentials
•Follow this workflow inside your AWS Management Console to generate your keys:

•Open AWS Account → Look at the top-right corner and click on your account name.
•From the dropdown menu, click on Security credentials.
•Scroll down the page and click the Create access key button.
•Set the appropriate permissions/use-case, and proceed to create the access key and secret key.

*Important: Copy the generated Access Key and Secret Access Key immediately, or download them as a .csv file as per your choice (you won't be able to view the secret key again after this step).
